{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And you shall make a table of shittim wood.</b> Most of the commentators agreed to say that the table corresponds to the crown of kingship, and the table of kings alludes to this. 'Table of shittim wood' (שֻׁלְחָן עֲצֵי שִׁטִּים) — the initial letters ascend to the number thirty, for the yod is absorbed; or one may also include the vav of 'and you shall make' (וְעָשִׂיתָ). And these four words allude to kingship, which is acquired through thirty traits, and this encompasses all human success for one whom Elohim has graced with it, for from this lofty table flows abundance and satiety of will to the entire world. And its being two cubits in length and one cubit in width, whole and not broken, is because ״יִתְרוֹן אֶרֶץ בַּכֹּל הִיא״ (Ecclesiastes 5:8), and all the good things of this world have an end and limit in length and breadth, as it is written (Genesis 13:17) ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ בָּאָרֶץ לְאָרְכָּהּ וּלְרָחְבָּהּ״. And above we wrote that this alludes to the trait of the righteous, who rejoice in their portion and lack nothing for themselves; and in all that the Holy One, blessed be He, feeds them from His table, whether much or little, it seems to them as though they have everything, as explained above in the portion of Vayishlaḥ at the verse ״יֶשׁ לִי כֹל״ (Genesis 33:11). Therefore the cubits of the table were whole in its length and breadth; but in the measure of its height it is written 'and a cubit and a half its height' — an allusion that one's heart should not be high nor lifted up on high because of his wealth, for the broken cubit in the height alludes that he should have a broken heart in any case, and not stand erect because his force is great." "And he further alluded that one who eats from a lofty table should not fill the bit of his belly and his craving, but should break his desire and not bring to completion all his wishes toward the material, for the righteous eats only to satisfy his soul, and the surplus he consecrates. And for this reason the language of breaking (שְׁבִירָה) is used in connection with bread: ״שִׁבְרוּ לָנוּ מְעַט אֹכֶל״ (Genesis 43:2), ״בְּשִׁבְרִי לָכֶם מַטֵּה לֶחֶם״ (Leviticus 26:26). And this is good for a person, both from the side of medicine — that one should not fill the stomach with as much food as it can bear, for this thing harms digestion, but rather should withhold about a third from satiety, and this is the foundation of all human health — and from the side of the commandments of HaShem, for there is nothing good for a person except to diminish in every capacity regarding human luxuries, so that he not be drawn after them and be neglectful of the business of Torah, for such is the way of Torah: ״פַּת בְּמֶלַח תֹּאכֵל״ (Avot 6:4)."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:17.768791935Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}